Hello, for the journal two, that was assigned as journal three because of the problem I had with the conference of the [expletive] institution, the [expletive] institute, sorry. I learned a lot of new, of some words stressed in a particular way in the entire sentence and its individual pronunciation. So, starting with "than," "than," it was interesting because I didn't know the correct pronunciation and I didn't know that reduced, it was reduced as "than," the same pronunciation. So, for example, "You are taller than me," "I was there than I went home." It's the same reduction, the same pronunciation because they are reduced in the same way. With "only," I didn't know that it was "o," that first sound, that diphthong, I didn't know, so sometimes I pronounced it like "only" or maybe "only," but not like "only," "only," it's "only," and that "i," the "i," "only," the ending "i." And "look," it's important because some people say "look," but it's "look," this relaxed "look," and it is important to stress it because it's a verb, either a verb or a noun. And the content words, I reviewed that the content words, the words that are stressed mostly in sentences that are verbs, adjectives, nouns, and verbs, right? So, I reviewed that, we were learning that in class. And "come," I learned that "come," that is relaxed, "come," "come," not "come," but "come," "come," very similar to "come," but "come," that is stressed because it's a verb. And "it's," "it's," it is expressing possession without an apostrophe, "it's," "it's," it's reduced in a very similar way to "it's" of "verb," but in the beginning of a sentence, sometimes Americans are going to say "ts," only "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts," "ts